For the course-of-values value of the sharp decider step, the consistency bit at a pair of indices is one exactly when some sharp neighbourhood lies in the intersection, the intersection component then equals that intersection, and the equality bit is one exactly when the two sharp neighbourhoods are equal, assuming D's deciders are correct and every neighbourhood of D is nonempty.

The argument is strong induction on the pair code, splitting each index into zero, an odd leaf, or an even node. A zero on the left returns the pack of one minus the other index, one, and that index: the intersection with the master neighbourhood is the other neighbourhood, so consistency is immediate, and equality holds only when the other index is the master, by the characterization of the master neighbourhood. A zero on the right against a leaf or a node is symmetric, and equality fails because the nonzero index is not the master. Two leaves transport D's consistency, intersection, and equality along the odd embedding, using injectivity of the leaf embedding for equality. A leaf against a node, or a node against a leaf, has empty intersection, so the zero bits are correct, a supposed witness contradicts nonemptiness, and equality would identify a leaf embedding with a node embedding. Two nodes have strictly smaller child pairs; the memo table returns the earlier packed values, and consistency, intersection, and equality are the conjunctions of the two inductive results, using injectivity of the node embedding.
